{"authored" [{"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f129727f06399c32b1c5555ffe5852562ff50df36beb95790acb546bab56b497" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae5f4614e9fc9ee28b805d5341752288718228ced4161363192c888bb147353" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "397e672e68fb91e2fddf39cda03f507c95d3dbfe23cd2549fe5c659ce7f28f09" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "397e672e68fb91e2fddf39cda03f507c95d3dbfe23cd2549fe5c659ce7f28f09" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "9f0afaa119ed23c739b16b3244fcdd961ade1a88f488b7328a9d704bebbeab1b" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "9f0afaa119ed23c739b16b3244fcdd961ade1a88f488b7328a9d704bebbeab1b" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "a78c187e405e594bf97414a03f18f859cf5a3865d8a743af18e296cbe3a67d73" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "a78c187e405e594bf97414a03f18f859cf5a3865d8a743af18e296cbe3a67d73" "basis" 1286 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038018/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-86901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4},\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-86901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {4}^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =12 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $12$ | $24$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-86901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-86901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-86901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-86901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,y(2)=e\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =e+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4) \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $e$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $e+4$ | $10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ | $5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10(e+4)"] ["math" "10\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)"] ["math" "5(e+4)"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)"] ["math" "e+4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-89084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(y+x)^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(y+x)^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351668" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-351668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351668" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | $\\,?\\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot e \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ | $\\frac{e}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{16}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{3e}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{64}{e}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{e}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-120842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{e}{16}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x\\sqrt{y},\\,y(4)=4\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $Δy$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $$ | $$ | $$ | {{field-1}} |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =4+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x\\sqrt{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{17}{4}$ | $6$ | $\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{6}$ | $\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{16}$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-348069" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{16}\\sqrt{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3y-2x,\\,y(2)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=2$ to $x=4$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3y-2x=3\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot 1=6 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $4$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117941" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={1}^{2}\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y={2}^{2}\\cdot 2=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot 1=8 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ | $8$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $10$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "13"] ["math" "14"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+y,\\,y(3)=2,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=3$ to $x=4$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be  \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-3}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(3)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=3+2=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+y=\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}=8 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{7}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{9}{2}+4=\\frac{17}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $5$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $8$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $\\frac{17}{2}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(4)≈\\frac{17}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{11}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{13}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{15}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{17}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-53864" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{y},\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =2+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x}{y}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{8}=\\frac{17}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{17}{8}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(1)≈\\frac{17}{8}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-351667" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351667" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{17}{8}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+4y,\\,y(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+10=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+4y=2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 12=52 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=52\\cdot 1=52 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=12+52=64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $12$ | $52$ | $52$ |\n| $3$ | $64$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈64.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "44"] ["math" "54"] ["math" "64"] ["math" "74"] ["math" "78"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-50091" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "64"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+5-y,\\,y(-1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(-1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=-1$ and $x=2$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-1)}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=-1+5-2=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=0+5-4=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+1=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+5-y=1+5-5=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=5+1=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351690" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-351690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351690" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(y-1),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(4).$ The distance from $x=1$ and $x=4$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-1}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=1\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=2\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-1)=3\\cdot (1-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table. \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $4$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(4)≈1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(2)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=2+2\\cdot (-1)=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=3+2\\cdot (-1)=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+2y=4+2\\cdot 0=4 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+4=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $4$ | $0$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $5$ | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2},\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-0}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-2.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+0=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-2)^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}{x}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $3$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-117968" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x+2y,\\,y(2)=-3\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(5).$\n\n$y(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(2)$ and asked to find $y(5).$ The distance from $x=2$ and $x=5$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-2}{3}=\\frac{3}{3}=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(2)=-3.$ \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(2)+2(-3)=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(3)+2(-1)=10 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=10\\cdot 1=10 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=3+1=4 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+10=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+2y=4(4)+2(9)=34 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=34\\cdot 1=34 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=4+1=5 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=9+34=43\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $34$ | $34$ |\n| $5$ | $43$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(5)≈43.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c8ffd3b73a637be18772fedda5b94875136b6ca5d10c471cefbbf11f8acf77ad"} {"question" "q-351692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "q-351692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "43"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/state.json" "file_sha256" "5855f09704bada11cd1be14fb4d3a0e7b63dc05d7311f3af3f876d3a492bbfcb"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny'=xy^4, \\qquad y(2) = 1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method. What entry should be placed in the column for $\\Delta y$ in the second row of the table below if the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\vphantom{1}$ |  |  | $\\, ? \\,$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "4d07039393097b3b8b1a51f3478dd463c15770e86f50bc06b700ea651cc8970d"} {"question" "e-7701" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $y'$ according to the given rule (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row), and we compute \n$\\Delta{y}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (2)^{4} \\\\  & =40 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =40\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.  \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n| $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ | $2$ | $40$ | $20$ |\n\nTherefore, the missing value is $\\Delta y = 20.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-7701.json" "file_sha256" "4d07039393097b3b8b1a51f3478dd463c15770e86f50bc06b700ea651cc8970d"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy, \\qquad y(1)=3,\n$$\n\nuse Euler's method to find an approximation to $y(2)$ using two steps of equal size." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "df1d469851fdaaae3d18659591cf40b5569e728f17a056a1f000eba16fe22f74"} {"question" "e-537" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(1)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2-1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=3.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3=6 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+3=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y' \\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2xy=2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 6=18 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=6+9=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\, y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $6$ | $18$ | $9$ |\n| $2$ | $15$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that  $y(2) \\approx 15.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-537.json" "file_sha256" "df1d469851fdaaae3d18659591cf40b5569e728f17a056a1f000eba16fe22f74"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = x^3-y^2, \\qquad y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with three steps of equal size to approximate $y(3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "f43590ada023baa8f9d85c0bccbf18f1667e9596fb8b6c791a2641c7bad39ec1"} {"question" "e-9354" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(3).$ The distance from $x=0$ and $x=3$ is $3,$ and since we are asked to use three steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{3-0}{3}=\\dfrac{3}{3} = 1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={0}^{3}-{1}^{2}=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\n \n\n**Step 2.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={1}^{3}-{0}^{2}=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot 1=1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+1=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 3.** We compute $y'$ and $\\Delta{y}$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the third row) and new values of $x_{\\text{new}}$ and $y_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}={2}^{3}-{1}^{2}=7 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=7\\cdot 1=7 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+7=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\, \\Delta y \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)\\approx 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040117/example-9354.json" "file_sha256" "f43590ada023baa8f9d85c0bccbf18f1667e9596fb8b6c791a2641c7bad39ec1"}] "usage" {"e-537" [] "e-9354" [] "q-117968" [] "e-7701" [] "q-53876" [] "q-348069" []} "basis" 1527 "retractions" [[17592186176446 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = x^{2}y, y(1) = 1$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(3)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186175219 17592186175220 17592186175221 17592186175222 17592186175223 17592186297977 17592186297978 17592186297979 17592186297980 17592186297981 17592186298005 17592186298006] "immutable_fields" [[17592186175218 :answer-field/correct] [17592186175218 :answer-field/id] [17592186175218 :answer-field/key] [17592186175218 :answer-field/type] [17592186297976 :answer-field/correct] [17592186297976 :answer-field/id] [17592186297976 :answer-field/key] [17592186297976 :answer-field/type] [17592186298004 :answer-field/correct] [17592186298004 :answer-field/id] [17592186298004 :answer-field/key] [17592186298004 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
